                  </story-point>
                  <story-point caption='Geographic disparity: Average petrol prices differ sharply by region. Europe and Oceania are highest in this dataset, while the Middle East is lowest.' captured-sheet='Sheet 2' id='2' />
                  <story-point caption='Income context: The dataset separates countries into income categories, and the price patterns differ across those groups. This comparison is descriptive; country income is not household affordability.' captured-sheet='Sheet 3' id='3' />
                  <story-point caption='Regional context: Country-level averages are grouped into seven regions. Differences in fuel price reflect many market and policy factors and should not be attributed to one category alone.' captured-sheet='Sheet 4' id='4' />
                  <story-point caption='Taxation: Country tax averages vary widely. The comparison shows association and does not establish that tax rates alone drive pump prices.' captured-sheet='Sheet 5' id='5' />
                  <story-point caption='Temporal trend: Petrol price averages rose from 2020 to 2022, eased in 2023, and were higher by April 2026. The 2026 value covers only part of the year.' captured-sheet='Global Petrol Trend' id='6' />
                  <story-point caption='Conclusion and recommendations: Use the regional and annual comparisons to identify where deeper country analysis is needed. Combine price and tax trends with current market data, and avoid treating these averages as a direct affordability or causal measure.' captured-sheet='Global Fuel Prices Dashboard' id='7' />
                </story-points>
